  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nThe core idea is to enhance the optimization process by combining kernel method properties for smooth function approximation with simulated annealing and multi-resolution search. Unlike the previous methods focused on local perturbations, we use a kernel representation to capture global structures that minimize the maximum shift correlation. This addresses the problem's non-convexity and boundary handling through a mathematically derived reward function integrating local features and integral constraints. The approach scales geometrically with the sample size and uses annealing to escape local optima.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\nRepresentation: h(x) = kernel expansion: h(x_i) = \u2211W_a \u03c8(x_i) + linear terms\nWhere \u03c8(x) is a piecewise exponential kernel base adapted for the interval [0,2]. W_a are weights initialized to model local approximations.\n\nConstraints:\n\u222bh(x)dx = 1 is enforced via linear constraints on the kernel expansion coefficients.\n0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 must hold for all x. This is enforced by projection after each update.\n\nObjective Function:\nC_5 = max_k (minimum overlap measure from local and integral estimates), defined as:\n  C_scaled = [max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx] * (2/n)\nThe function includes:\n  Local features (9-term expansion) and global fit (Fourier basis terms)\n\nWe use a penalized optimization with continuous variables to maintain feasibility.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\nInitialize:\n- Sample n_points = 1024 (resampled parent if provided)\n- Initialize kernel points and coefficients W_a from parent data\n- Define explab and regularization parameters\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Simulated annealing with adaptive kernel width\n- At each step:\n  - Sample new solution from nearby kernel points\n  - Evaluate objective using a hybrid local-aware approach\n  - Accept move if C_5 improves or by Metropolis criterion\n- Post-processing:\n  - Project solution to satisfy [0,1] and integral constraints\n  - Refine locally with conjugate gradient steps\n\nHelper functions:\n- gaussian_kernel(x)\n- project_feasible(w,n)\n- compute_c(w,n)\n- kernel_matrix(X, \u03c3)\n- neighborhood_sample(w)\n\nThe core innovation is the multi-scale, penalized kernel-based sampling approach to handle boundary conditions and global shifts.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n- Base kernel width: \u03c3 = 0.1, annealed to 0.95^t\n- Fraction of feasible solutions kept: 0.3\n- Normal mode pooling: enable/disable flag\n- Local refinement level: 7\n- Automatic pedestrian regeneration: true\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n- Main annealing schedule runs for 800 steps\n- Initial setup: n=256 (compute light)\n- Benchmark: 1e-2 error tolerance per step\n- Fallback mechanism: keep best solution after n failures\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n- Always verify [0,1] and integral after every update\n- Self-check: compare scores with previous record\n- Project solution at encode stage\n- Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n- Ensure C_5_\ud83d\udcafhundredths(m$ million)digit accuracy",
